From: GapKeep <noreply@gapkeep.org>
Subject: usb: leave a low-speed device behind a full-speed hub alone

A DigiMacro (V-USB, low speed) behind a cheap hub that only runs at full
speed ("USB2.0 HUB", a05:7211) took the whole USB bus down, at power-on
and when plugged in later: the keyboard on the same hub was lost too,
until the next reboot (seen on the rig, 2026-11-13).

Such a hub has no transaction translator, so the low-speed device is
reached with PRE packets on a full-speed root port. A trace of the
channel and port registers showed the first SETUP to the device end in a
transaction error, with the root port disabled by the controller itself
(HPRT 0x21405 -> 0x21401); no frames went out after that, and every other
request timed out. Linux's dwc2 driver refuses the same case for the same
reason (from NetBSD: low-speed packets on a full-speed root port make it
stop responding).

CUSBDevice::Initialize() now gives up on a low-speed device behind a hub
when there is no split transfer (no high-speed hub with a translator on
the way), before sending it anything, and says why. The hub and its
other devices keep working. A high-speed (USB 2.0) hub, or plugging the
device in directly, is unaffected. Pi 1-3 and Zero only (DWC OTG); xHCI
handles this in hardware.

--- a/lib/usb/usbdevice.cpp
+++ b/lib/usb/usbdevice.cpp
@@ -177,10 +177,28 @@
 	}
 #endif
 
+#if RASPPI <= 3
+	// gapkeep: a low-speed device behind a hub that runs at full speed (no
+	// transaction translator, so no split transfers) is reached with PRE
+	// packets on a full-speed root port. The DWC OTG controller can't do
+	// that: its root port disables itself at the first such packet, and
+	// every other device on the bus is lost with it (the keyboard too).
+	// Leave this one device alone; a high-speed (USB 2.0) hub works.
+	if (   m_Speed == USBSpeedLow
+	    && m_pHub != 0
+	    && !m_bSplitTransfer)
+	{
+		LogWrite (LogError, "Low-speed device behind a full-speed hub: not supported"
+				    " by this USB controller (use a USB 2.0 hub, or plug it in directly)");
+
+		return FALSE;
+	}
+#endif
+
 	assert (m_pDeviceDesc == 0);
 	m_pDeviceDesc = new TUSBDeviceDescriptor;
 	assert (m_pDeviceDesc != 0);
-	
+
 	assert (m_pHost != 0);
 	assert (m_pEndpoint0 != 0);
 	
